    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d662">

                <pb facs="0890" n="820" sameAs="frus:frus1862/pg820"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr.
                        Pruyn.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 10.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1862-03-24"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >March</hi> 24, 1862.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Mr. Harris’s despatch of November 23 (No.
                    49) has been received. The views expressed therein concerning the questions
                    between this government and that of Japan, which had previously been committed
                    to him for consideration, <pb facs="0891" n="821" xml:id="pg821"/> appear to the
                    President to be eminently sagacious. You will communicate these sentiments to
                    Mr. Harris if he shall yet be remaining at Yedo.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert H. Pruyn,</hi> Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c., Yedo.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
